WITH ins AS (INSERT INTO sais_resource_deadlines (resource_id, appeal_deadline, continued_benefits_deadline, hearing_request_deadline, reconsideration_deadline, judicial_review_deadline, source_file, source_hash8) VALUES 
('SAIS-PS-002-02', 'Not an appeal posture. Safety planning is continuous and is revised as circumstances change -- revisit it after any escalation, any court date, and any move.', 'Do not delay a protective order or shelter placement in order to finish assembling documents. Documents are replaceable through the pipelines in the Civil Documents family; safety is not.', 'Ask an advocate for a LETHALITY OR DANGER ASSESSMENT. Many jurisdictions use a structured screening tool, and a high-risk result can accelerate access to shelter, prosecution attention, and high-risk-team coordination.', 'Revisit the plan whenever anything changes -- a filing, a hearing, a job change, a move, a new relationship, or a release from custody. Plans made for a prior situation frequently do not fit the current one.', 'Not applicable. Safety planning runs alongside every legal and housing track here.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-002-03', 'Where a records-sealing or address-suppression request is denied, ask for the ruling in writing and renew the request with specific safety facts. Appeal deadlines are state-specific, commonly 30 days.', 'Enroll in the address confidentiality program BEFORE relocating. The program''s value is almost entirely preventive -- it substitutes an address going forward and cannot remove one already published.', 'Request address protection AT THE TIME OF FILING any court case, including protective order, custody, and divorce filings. Courts routinely grant it when asked and routinely do not raise it.', 'Where a landlord, employer, school, or agency has disclosed your location, ask for the disclosure in writing and raise it with the state DV coalition and, for funded programs, as a VAWA confidentiality violation.', 'Not applicable. Confidentiality work is continuous and should be revisited after every move, filing, or account change.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-002-04', 'Eviction judgment appeal: state-specific and very short, commonly 5-30 days, and frequently requiring a bond or ongoing rent deposit. Administrative grievance hearings in subsidized housing are commonly 10-14 days from the notice.', 'APPEARING ON THE COURT DATE IS THE CONTINUED-TENANCY MECHANISM. Non-appearance produces an immediate default judgment for possession. VAWA protections can be raised as a defense AT the hearing even if not asserted earlier.', 'Request an EMERGENCY TRANSFER in writing and ask for the provider''s written emergency transfer plan -- covered providers are required to have one. Request a grievance hearing within the window stated in any termination notice, commonly 10-14 days.', 'Where a provider denies VAWA protection, request the denial in writing with the reason. Then file with HUD and the state fair housing agency. Refusal to accept self-certification is a recurring and correctable violation.', 'HUD fair housing complaint: 1 year from the last discriminatory act. Private Fair Housing Act suit: 2 years. These run separately -- a HUD filing does not extend the court deadline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-002-05', 'Crime victim compensation denial: administrative appeal, commonly 30 days from the notice. Denials for a missed reporting window are frequently reversed on a good-cause showing -- appeal them.', 'Benefit continuation rules apply where existing assistance is being reduced or terminated. For Medicaid, requesting a fair hearing within the state''s continuation window -- commonly 10 DAYS -- generally keeps services in place pending decision.', 'Unemployment claim: file immediately on separation and state the DV reason explicitly. Appeal windows for unemployment denials are among the shortest in benefits law, commonly 10-30 days from the determination.', 'Request the TANF FAMILY VIOLENCE OPTION and the child support GOOD CAUSE EXEMPTION in writing and by name. Both exist in most states, both waive requirements that can be dangerous, and neither is routinely offered.', 'Judicial review of a final benefit determination follows the program''s appeal ladder, commonly 30 days from the final administrative decision.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-002-06', 'School placement disputes under McKinney-Vento: the district must enroll the child IMMEDIATELY pending dispute resolution, and provide written notice of the right to appeal. Appeal timelines are state-specific and short.', 'The child stays enrolled and attending DURING any dispute. This is the continued-service mechanism under McKinney-Vento and it is frequently ignored by districts until the statute is cited.', 'Contact the district HOMELESS LIAISON by name -- every district is required to have one, and going through the liaison rather than the front office resolves most enrollment problems immediately.', 'Where a program denies access based on disability, a service animal, a child''s age or gender, or language, request the denial in writing. Funded programs are subject to federal nondiscrimination requirements.', 'VAWA self-petitions, U visas, and T visas have no deadline tied to the abuse, but a U visa requires a law enforcement certification that becomes harder to obtain as time passes.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-003-01', 'Not an appeal posture. Criminal stalking limitations periods are state-specific; civil protection order petitions may be filed at any time and are strengthened by a longer documented pattern.', 'Continue logging even after a protective order issues. Post-order incidents are violations, and violations are what support enforcement, renewal, and any escalation to felony charges.', 'Report each incident to law enforcement and get an INCIDENT NUMBER each time, even when nothing appears to happen. A string of numbered reports is what converts ''she keeps complaining'' into a documented course of conduct.', 'Where an officer declines to take a report, ask for a supervisor and ask that the report be taken for documentation purposes. Declining to document is not the same as declining to charge, and you are entitled to the record.', 'Where the conduct crosses state lines or uses interstate electronic means, federal stalking statutes apply and the FBI may take a report. This is in addition to, not instead of, the state route.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-003-02', 'Not an appeal posture. Where a prosecutor declines to charge, request the declination in writing and ask the victim-witness unit what additional evidence would change the decision.', 'Continue reporting after any order issues. Post-order contact is a violation and is generally easier to charge than the underlying stalking, because the order supplies the notice element.', 'Report each incident promptly. Delay weakens both the fear element and the evidence, and some electronic records are retained only briefly -- see the preservation entry.', 'Where police decline to act, ask for a supervisor, then the detective bureau, then the prosecutor''s victim-witness unit. Advocates can frequently open doors a complainant cannot -- call your state coalition.', 'Federal stalking under 18 U.S.C. sec. 2261A covers interstate travel and use of interstate electronic communication. Report to the FBI or through IC3 where the conduct fits, in addition to the state report.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-003-03', 'Not an appeal posture. Preservation is time-critical and generally irreversible once retention expires -- there is no appeal from deleted data.', 'SCREENSHOT BEFORE REPORTING TO A PLATFORM. Platform removal is the most common way survivors accidentally destroy their own evidence.', 'Surveillance video: send the written preservation request within DAYS. Commercial and residential systems commonly overwrite in 7-30 days and the footage cannot be recovered afterward.', 'Where a provider or business declines a preservation request from you directly, ask law enforcement to send it. Providers respond to law enforcement preservation requests as a matter of routine.', 'Federal law permits providers to be required to preserve records pending legal process. This is initiated by law enforcement, not by the victim -- which is why asking for it explicitly matters.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-003-04', 'Not an appeal posture. Unauthorized access to accounts and unauthorized tracking are separately criminal in most states and federally -- report them as their own offenses, not just as evidence.', 'DO NOT REMOVE TRACKING OR CHANGE PASSWORDS BEFORE PLANNING THE SEQUENCE. Discovery is an escalation point. Coordinate with an advocate and law enforcement so the change happens alongside protective measures rather than before them.', 'Where a tracker or spyware is found, report it as a separate criminal act. Unauthorized electronic tracking and unauthorized computer access carry their own charges in most states.', 'Change account RECOVERY settings, not just passwords. Recovery email and phone numbers pointing at the other person silently restore their access after every reset -- this is the most common reason lockouts fail.', 'Federal offenses may apply to interstate electronic stalking, unauthorized access, and interception of communications. Report to the FBI or IC3 in addition to local law enforcement.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-003-05', 'Appeal of a denied petition: state-specific, commonly 30 days. Refiling with a more detailed chronology is usually faster and more effective than appealing.', 'THE TEMPORARY ORDER DISSOLVES IF YOU DO NOT APPEAR AT THE FULL HEARING, commonly 10-21 days out. Calendar it the moment the temporary order issues -- this is the most common way protection is lost.', 'Full hearing: commonly 10-21 days after the ex parte order. Bring the log, screenshots with timestamps and URLs visible, incident numbers, and witnesses. Ask for safety accommodations in advance.', 'Where relief you requested is omitted from the granted order, raise it before leaving the courtroom. Ask specifically for a bar on surveillance and tracking, which is frequently omitted from standard forms.', 'Orders commonly run 1-5 years and are generally RENEWABLE. Calendar the expiration and file for renewal before it lapses. Under 18 U.S.C. sec. 2265 the order is enforceable nationwide without registration.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-003-06', 'Crime victim compensation denial: administrative appeal, commonly 30 days. Title IX outcomes have institution-specific appeal windows, commonly 5-15 business days -- read the notice the day it arrives.', 'Title IX SUPPORTIVE MEASURES must be available whether or not a formal complaint is filed and regardless of any police report. Request them immediately; they do not require the investigation to conclude.', 'Crime victim compensation: commonly 1-3 years to apply, with a shorter law enforcement reporting requirement -- frequently 72 hours to 5 days. Good-cause exceptions are common and frequently granted.', 'Where an employer, school, or landlord refuses an accommodation, request the refusal in writing with the reason. That document is the basis for every escalation that follows.', 'HUD fair housing complaint: 1 year from the last act. Private Fair Housing Act suit: 2 years. Title IX complaints to the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights: generally 180 days from the last act of discrimination.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-004-01', 'Appeal of a denied petition: state-specific, commonly 30 days. BUT REFILING IS USUALLY FASTER AND BETTER THAN APPEALING -- a denial on an insufficient petition can generally be cured by refiling with more specific incidents, dates, and details.', 'THE TEMPORARY ORDER EXPIRES AT THE FULL HEARING. If you do not appear at that hearing, the temporary order generally dissolves and you are left with no protection. Calendar the hearing date the moment the temporary order is granted -- this is the single most common way protection is lost.', 'FULL HEARING: commonly 10-21 days after the ex parte order. You must appear. Bring your evidence, your witnesses, and a copy of the temporary order. If you need more time or the other party was not served, ASK FOR A CONTINUANCE AND ASK THAT THE TEMPORARY ORDER BE EXTENDED -- both in the same sentence.', 'If a petition is denied, ask the clerk what was insufficient and refile with specific dates, times, places, what was said and done, any injuries, any witnesses, and any prior incidents. Vagueness, not merit, is the usual reason for denial.', 'Full orders commonly last 1-5 years depending on the state and many are RENEWABLE. Calendar the expiration date and file for renewal BEFORE it lapses -- renewal is generally far easier than obtaining a new order.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-004-02', 'Appeal of a denial: commonly 30 days, but refiling with a more specific petition is generally the faster and more effective route.', 'If you are still in the home and it is safe to do so, gather identity documents, medications, keys, and financial records BEFORE filing, because the other party will be served and served papers change behavior. If it is not safe, do not delay the order for the documents.', 'Request a victim advocate at the courthouse -- most jurisdictions have one, they are free, and they help complete the petition. Call 800-799-7233 to be connected before you go if possible.', 'Where a request is denied but the order is granted in part, you may generally move to amend or raise the omitted relief at the full hearing. Note what was left out and raise it specifically.', 'Full order duration is set at the hearing and commonly runs 1-5 years. Ask the judge for the longest term available in your state, and ask what the renewal procedure is before you leave.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-004-03', 'Appeal of a denial of firearm relief: state-specific, commonly 30 days. More practically, raise it again at any modification or renewal hearing.', 'REPORT ANY VIOLATION IMMEDIATELY TO LAW ENFORCEMENT. Possession in violation of a qualifying order is a federal crime and is separately a state crime in most jurisdictions. Do not assume anyone else has reported it.', 'AT THE FULL HEARING, ask on the record for the credible-threat finding and the physical-force language. This is the single most consequential sentence spoken at most protective order hearings and it takes ten seconds.', 'Where surrender was ordered, ask whether a PROOF OF SURRENDER or compliance declaration was filed and by when. Where it was not, ask the court to set a compliance hearing -- unenforced surrender orders are common.', 'Firearm prohibition continues as long as the qualifying order is in effect. When the order nears expiration, the prohibition lapses with it -- another reason to calendar the renewal date well in advance.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-004-04', 'Appeal of a finding on an enforcement or contempt motion: state-specific, commonly 30 days. Reporting a new violation is generally faster than appealing an old ruling.', 'IF SERVICE HAS NOT OCCURRED BEFORE THE FULL HEARING, appear anyway and ask for a CONTINUANCE AND AN EXTENSION OF THE TEMPORARY ORDER in the same request. Do not let the temporary order lapse because service failed.', 'Report violations to law enforcement immediately and get an incident number each time. Separately, you may file a motion for contempt or enforcement with the issuing court -- the criminal and civil tracks run independently.', 'Where an officer declines to enforce because the order is from another state, state plainly that federal law requires enforcement under 18 U.S.C. sec. 2265 and ask for a supervisor. This is a recurring and correctable problem.', 'Modification or renewal: file with the ISSUING court before expiration. Only the issuing court can generally modify its own order; other states enforce but do not modify.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-004-05', 'Appeal of the full order or its terms: state-specific, commonly 30 days from entry. Motions to modify may generally be filed at any time on changed circumstances.', 'NON-APPEARANCE GENERALLY DISSOLVES THE TEMPORARY ORDER AND ENDS THE CASE. If you cannot attend, contact the clerk BEFORE the hearing and request a continuance in writing -- do not simply not appear.', 'Request safety accommodations in advance: separate waiting area, staggered departure, remote or screened testimony, a courthouse escort. These exist in most courts and are provided on request.', 'Where the order is granted but omits relief you asked for, raise it before leaving the courtroom. Correcting an order later requires a motion; correcting it in the moment takes a sentence.', 'Ask for the LONGEST TERM AVAILABLE in your state and write down the expiration date and the renewal procedure before you leave the building. Renewal is far easier than starting over.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-004-06', 'Crime victim compensation: state-specific, commonly 1-3 years to apply with a shorter reporting requirement -- frequently 72 hours to 5 days. Ask about good-cause exceptions, which are common.', 'Enroll in the Address Confidentiality Program BEFORE relocating where possible. Records already published are difficult to unwind, and the program''s value is largely preventive.', 'Request employer accommodations and job-protected leave in writing where your state provides it. Several states also prohibit termination or discrimination based on survivor status.', 'Where compensation is denied for a missed reporting deadline, appeal -- commonly 30 days -- and raise good cause. These denials are frequently reversed.', 'VAWA self-petitions, U visas, and T visas have no deadline tied to the abuse itself, but evidence degrades and a U visa requires a law enforcement certification that becomes harder to obtain over time.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-005-01', 'Denial appeal: administrative, state-specific, commonly 30 days from the denial notice. APPEAL RATHER THAN ABANDONING -- a large share of denials are for curable documentation problems rather than ineligibility.', 'Emergency awards are available in most states for immediate needs -- funeral costs, relocation, medical care -- BEFORE the full claim is decided. Ask for an EMERGENCY OR EXPEDITED AWARD by name at the time of application.', 'Application deadline: commonly 1-3 years from the crime, with a SHORTER law enforcement reporting requirement -- frequently 72 hours to 5 days. GOOD-CAUSE EXCEPTIONS to both are common and frequently granted.', 'Where a claim is denied, request the specific basis in writing and ask what documentation would change the outcome. Most programs allow reconsideration on new documentation without a formal appeal.', 'Judicial review of a final administrative decision: state-specific, commonly 30 days. Most claims resolve at reconsideration or administrative appeal and never reach court.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-005-02', 'Denial or partial award appeal: state-specific, commonly 30 days from the notice. Partial awards can generally be supplemented later as additional bills arrive -- ask about keeping the claim open.', 'Ask for an EMERGENCY AWARD for immediate needs while the full claim is processed. Most states can issue funds for funeral costs, relocation, or urgent medical care within days.', 'Submit bills and receipts as they arrive rather than waiting for a total. Most programs accept supplemental submissions on an open claim, and waiting risks the filing deadline.', 'Where a category is denied as not covered, ask for the specific statutory or rule basis. Coverage categories are frequently broader than the intake form suggests, particularly for relocation and security measures.', 'Where a program pays and the offender is later ordered to pay restitution, the program is generally subrogated to that amount. This does not reduce what you receive -- it means the program collects from the offender, not from you.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-005-03', 'Denial for untimeliness: appeal within the stated window, commonly 30 days, and RAISE GOOD CAUSE EXPRESSLY with supporting documentation. These denials are among the most frequently reversed.', 'File the application as soon as possible even with incomplete documentation. Most programs will hold an incomplete claim open pending records rather than reject it -- filing stops the clock.', 'Reporting deadline: commonly 72 hours to 5 days, longer for sexual assault, child abuse, and elder abuse. Ask which ALTERNATIVE reports your state accepts -- forensic exam, APS or CPS report, protective order petition, or Title IX report.', 'Where a late claim is denied, request reconsideration with a written good-cause statement and supporting documentation -- medical records, a therapist letter, a protective order, or an advocate declaration.', 'Minors: the application clock frequently runs from the eighteenth birthday rather than from the crime, which means claims believed long expired are frequently still live. Verify this specifically.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-005-04', 'Denial appeal: commonly 30 days from the notice. CONTRIBUTORY CONDUCT findings in particular are frequently reversed on appeal where the applicant was defending themselves or was a survivor of ongoing abuse -- appeal them.', 'Ask about an EMERGENCY OR EXPEDITED AWARD at intake for urgent needs. Do not wait for the full claim decision to raise it -- most programs will not offer it unprompted.', 'Submit the application on time even if documentation is incomplete. Programs generally hold claims open pending records, and filing preserves the deadline.', 'Where documentation is the issue, request a specific written list of what is missing and resubmit. Documentation denials are the most common and the most curable category.', 'Where the program requires cooperation with prosecution and you have safety concerns about participating, say so in writing and ask how your state handles safety-based non-cooperation -- most have a provision for it.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-005-05', 'Appeal of a restitution order or amount: through the criminal case, and generally only by the parties. Raise disputes with the prosecutor BEFORE sentencing, when the amount is being determined.', 'Register with the victim notification system in the case so you receive notice of hearings, plea agreements, release, and any change in custody status. Ask the victim-witness unit to enroll you.', 'SUBMIT THE LOSS STATEMENT BEFORE SENTENCING. The court orders restitution based on documented loss presented at that point; adding losses afterward is difficult and sometimes impossible.', 'Where restitution was ordered but is not being paid, contact the prosecutor''s office and the collections unit. Orders are enforceable as judgments and non-payment can be a probation or parole violation.', 'Restitution orders remain enforceable for many years and generally survive the offender''s bankruptcy. Keep your contact information current with the court for the life of the order.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-005-06', 'Each route has its own appeal path and deadline. Benefit denials commonly allow 30-90 days; unemployment appeals are among the shortest, commonly 10-30 days from the determination.', 'For Medicaid and other continuing benefits, requesting a fair hearing within the state''s continuation window -- commonly 10 DAYS -- generally keeps existing services in place pending decision.', 'Civil limitations periods are commonly 1-3 years and many states extend or toll them for crime victims or during an ongoing prosecution. Confirm your state''s rule before assuming a claim is time-barred.', 'Where a third party''s negligence may have enabled the crime, send a written EVIDENCE PRESERVATION demand immediately -- surveillance video and incident reports are retained briefly and are the core of a premises case.', 'U visa certification from law enforcement becomes harder to obtain as time passes and as personnel change. Request it early if it may be relevant.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-007-01', 'Not an appeal posture for a victim. Where a prosecutor declines the bias enhancement, request the declination in writing and refer separately to the FBI and the state Attorney General''s civil rights unit -- federal and state charging authority is independent.', 'Preserve everything before cleanup. Graffiti, damaged property, and left materials are the physical evidence of motive and are frequently removed within hours by well-meaning people -- photograph extensively first.', 'Report to local law enforcement AND the FBI. The FBI is the lead federal investigative agency for hate crimes, and a federal investigation can proceed regardless of local charging decisions.', 'Where police record the incident without noting bias, ask in writing that the report be amended to reflect the bias indicators, and list them specifically. Reports without a bias flag do not enter hate crime tracking or trigger specialized review.', 'Federal hate crime prosecutions are brought by DOJ and do not depend on state charges. State limitations periods for the base offense apply to the state case; federal periods apply independently.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-007-02', 'Where a report was taken without bias indicators, request a written amendment or supplemental report listing them. Do this promptly -- amendments are much harder once a case has been closed or declined.', 'Do not clean up, repaint, or repair before the evidence is photographed and, ideally, before law enforcement has documented it. Ask the property owner in writing to delay remediation.', 'Report the same day where possible. Surveillance video from nearby businesses and residences is typically overwritten in 7-30 DAYS -- ask law enforcement to collect it and send your own written preservation request.', 'Where police decline to classify the incident as bias-motivated, ask for a supervisor, then contact the FBI and the state Attorney General''s civil rights unit directly. Classification by the local agency does not bind them.', 'Federal investigation may proceed independently of the state case. Civil claims under federal and state civil rights statutes run on their own limitations periods, commonly 1-4 years.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-007-03', 'Where a prosecutor declines the bias enhancement, request the declination in writing. Then refer separately to the FBI and the state AG -- their charging authority is independent of the local prosecutor''s.', 'Report promptly to preserve the parallel civil track. Administrative civil rights deadlines are substantially shorter than criminal limitations periods and run while the criminal case proceeds.', 'State human rights commission or EEOC filing: commonly 180 DAYS, extended to 300 where a state or local agency has concurrent jurisdiction. HUD fair housing complaint: 1 YEAR. These run independently of any criminal case.', 'If the incident was recorded without bias classification, request supervisory review and amendment. Separately ask the FBI to review -- federal classification does not depend on the local agency''s characterization.', 'Federal civil rights lawsuits generally must be filed within the state''s analogous personal injury period, commonly 1-4 years. Private Fair Housing Act suit: 2 years. Title VII suit: 90 days after right-to-sue.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-007-04', 'Administrative civil rights complaints must generally be filed first for employment and housing claims: 180-300 days for employment, 1 year for HUD fair housing. Missing these can foreclose the court claim.', 'Send a written EVIDENCE PRESERVATION demand to any landlord, employer, school, or business involved immediately. Their video, incident files, and prior complaints about the same person are the core of an institutional claim and are retained briefly.', 'After an EEOC right-to-sue notice: 90 DAYS to file in court. This is the hardest deadline in employment law and is missed constantly.', 'Where an institution denies responsibility, request its written policy, its investigation file, and any prior complaints about the same person. A documented pattern the institution knew about transforms the claim.', 'Federal civil rights suits generally borrow the state''s personal injury limitations period, commonly 1-4 years. Private Fair Housing Act suit: 2 years from the last discriminatory act.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-007-05', 'Crime victim compensation denial: administrative appeal, commonly 30 days. Denials for a missed reporting window are frequently reversed on good cause -- appeal them.', 'Ask for an EMERGENCY OR EXPEDITED compensation award for relocation, security measures, or urgent medical care while the full claim is processed. Most states can act within days.', 'Compensation application: commonly 1-3 years with a shorter reporting requirement, frequently 72 hours to 5 days. Good-cause exceptions exist in every state.', 'Where a compensation category is denied, ask for the specific basis. Relocation and security measures are covered in many states and are among the least-claimed categories.', 'Nonprofit security grant applications run on annual cycles with fixed windows. Ask the state administrative agency when the next cycle opens rather than assuming it is closed.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-007-06', 'Administrative appeal of a commission or agency dismissal: agency-specific, commonly 30-90 days. OCR and HUD determinations have their own reconsideration procedures.', 'Request INTERIM RELIEF while a complaint is pending -- schools must provide supportive measures, housing providers may be asked for a transfer, and employers may be asked for schedule or assignment changes.', 'EEOC or state fair employment agency: 180 DAYS, or 300 with concurrent state jurisdiction. HUD fair housing: 1 YEAR. ED Office for Civil Rights: generally 180 DAYS. These are the shortest clocks in this pipeline and they run silently.', 'Where a complaint is dismissed for insufficient information, request the specific basis and refile with the missing detail. Administrative dismissals are frequently curable rather than final.', 'AFTER AN EEOC RIGHT-TO-SUE NOTICE: 90 DAYS TO FILE IN COURT. Private Fair Housing Act suit: 2 years from the last discriminatory act, running independently of any HUD filing.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-009-01', 'Appeal of a denial or inadequacy of supportive measures: institution-specific and short, commonly 5-15 business days from the decision. Read the notice the day it arrives.', 'SUPPORTIVE MEASURES CONTINUE WHETHER OR NOT A COMPLAINT PROCEEDS. If the school withdraws them because you declined to file, object in writing -- they are not conditioned on participation in a grievance process.', 'Request in writing to the Title IX Coordinator. There is no deadline to request supportive measures and no requirement to have reported to police, but request early -- academic remedies are easier before grades post.', 'Where measures are inadequate, request modification in writing and specify what would work. Schools must periodically reassess, and a documented request that goes unanswered is the foundation of any OCR complaint.', 'ED Office for Civil Rights complaint: generally 180 DAYS from the last act of discrimination. This runs independently of any campus process and is not extended by the school''s internal timeline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-PS-009-02', 'Complaint about a school''s failure to provide required notification: file with ED Office for Civil Rights, generally within 180 DAYS, and separately as a Clery Act complaint, which has no fixed deadline.', 'Request the written notification even after the fact. Receiving it late still tells you what is available, and the delay is documented evidence of the school''s noncompliance.', 'Ask the Title IX Coordinator in writing for the notification document and for the school''s grievance procedures. Both must be published and both must be provided on request.', 'Where the school claims it provided notice and you never received it, request proof of delivery. Schools are required to document these notifications.', 'Clery Act violations are enforced by the Department of Education and can result in substantial fines and loss of federal funding. Complaints may be filed at any time.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18')
ON CONFLICT (resource_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING resource_id) SELECT count(*) AS inserted FROM ins